---
import ranking from "../data/ranking.json";
import showcase from "../data/showcase.json";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { rupees } from "../lib/format";

const { stats } = ranking;
const { plan } = showcase;
const perTree = rupees(plan.cost_rs / plan.trees);
const issue = "https://github.com/riteshroshann/neem/issues/new?title=Partnering%20with%20Neem";
---

<Base
  title="Docs"
  description="Developer documentation for Neem: how the data, the SOLWEIG physics, the instant preview, the child model and the planning agent fit together, how to run and extend it, and how NGOs and funders can use it."
  path="/docs"
  night
>
  <section class="wrap head page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Developer docs</p>
    <h1 class="display docs-title">Build on Neem.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Neem is a handful of small programs passing files to each other. This page walks through them
      in the order the data flows: what each one does, why it exists, and how to run it, change it
      and take it further. Plain words, real commands.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc small" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#flow">The flow</a>
      <a href="#physics">Physics</a>
      <a href="#instant">Instant</a>
      <a href="#child">The child</a>
      <a href="#agent">The agent</a>
      <a href="#cloud">The cloud</a>
      <a href="#run">Run it</a>
      <a href="#api">Files and API</a>
      <a href="#worlds">Worlds</a>
      <a href="#extend">Extend it</a>
      <a href="#further">How far</a>
      <a href="#partners">NGOs and funders</a>
    </nav>
  </section>

  <article class="wrap body">
    <section class="block rule" id="flow">
      <h2 class="title">The whole thing in one breath</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Open data goes in: buildings, tree heights, ground height, schools and weather. A scene
          builder cuts a 1 m grid around each school. SOLWEIG, a radiation model, works out how hot
          every square metre feels for every hour of the school day. A model of a child's body turns
          that into a core temperature and the water lost. A fast model in your browser predicts what
          trees would change, and SOLWEIG checks it. An agent writes plans with tools that can't lie
          about numbers. Everything ends up as plain files that the website reads.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow num">
          <li><b>ingest</b> Overture, Meta canopy, Copernicus, OpenStreetMap, ERA5</li>
          <li><b>scene</b> a 1 m grid per school: ground, roofs, crowns, what counts as yard</li>
          <li><b>solweig</b> mean radiant temperature, then UTCI, hour by hour</li>
          <li><b>twin</b> the child's morning, and the files the planner needs</li>
          <li><b>publish</b> ranking, maps and twins into <code>web/</code></li>
          <li><b>web</b> the site, the planner and its instant preview</li>
          <li><b>api + worker</b> "Verify with physics": a real SOLWEIG run on your design</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="physics">
      <h2 class="title">Physics, from the ground up</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A thermometer in the shade reads the air. A child in an open yard is not in the shade. They
          take in direct sun, the glare of the sky, sunlight bouncing off the ground, and heat
          glowing off every surface that has been baking since morning. Add all of that up and you
          get the <b>mean radiant temperature</b>: the temperature of an imaginary room that would
          bathe you in the same radiation. In a Delhi yard at 1 pm it runs 20 to 25 °C above the air.
        </p>
        <p>
          SOLWEIG computes it for a person standing on every cell of the grid. It casts shadows from
          roofs and crowns (crowns let a little light through), measures how much sky each cell can
          see, and sums the short- and long-wave radiation arriving from six directions. Then
          <b>UTCI</b> folds in air temperature, humidity and wind to give one "feels like" number on
          a scale built around the human body. Above 46 °C is extreme heat stress. We run UMEP's Rust
          implementation on a laptop GPU: about four seconds for a 250 m school.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="instant">
      <h2 class="title">Making it instant</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Four seconds is fast for physics and slow for a mouse. The trick is to predict the
          <i>change</i>, not the temperature. Squares your design doesn't touch keep SOLWEIG's exact
          value. Where you plant, the browser recasts shadows from the new crowns and applies a small
          model of how much a square cools when it goes from sun to shade, fitted per hour to
          SOLWEIG runs of that same school. It takes about 10 ms.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then we check it. Tests set the browser's shadow caster against SOLWEIG (they agree on more
          than 97% of cells) and the preview against full runs of designs it never saw (under 1 °C
          off on average). Right under a new crown the preview can be a few degrees off at midday,
          and the page tells you so. "Verify with physics" sends your design to a worker that reruns
          SOLWEIG and the child's day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="child">
      <h2 class="title">A child, not an average</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          JOS-3 models a body as 17 segments, each with core, muscle, fat and skin, and simulates
          sweating and blood flow minute by minute. We scale it to a nine-year-old (1.32 m, 28 kg)
          and walk it through a government school's morning shift: assembly outdoors, class, recess,
          the walk home. Outdoors it stands in the yard's typical radiant heat for that hour.
        </p>
        <p>
          JOS-3 was built from adults, so its set points don't scale down to children. Neem reports
          what holds up: the rise above the child's own resting state, and the water lost. Treat them
          as indicative, not clinical.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="agent">
      <h2 class="title">An agent that can't make numbers up</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The planner is a Strands agent running qwen3 4B through Ollama, on the same laptop. It
          gets a school, a budget and five tools: read the brief, plant trees, add a shade sail,
          preview, verify. The model decides how many trees, which species and whether a sail is
          worth it. The tools decide where (the hottest open ground, a crown's width apart), refuse
          to overspend, and compute every number. If the model forgets to verify, the code does it
          anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rule is simple: a language model may choose, but only physics may report. That is what
          makes it safe to put its plans in front of a principal.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="cloud">
      <h2 class="title">The cloud, on a laptop</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>infra/template.yaml</code> is an AWS SAM template for the whole service: an API on
          Lambda, a worker on SQS, a Step Functions map that ranks schools on up to a hundred workers
          at once, DynamoDB for jobs and S3 for the data lake. LocalStack emulates all of it, so
          <code>sam local</code> runs the same Lambda images on your machine. Cedar holds the rules:
          anyone may explore, physics runs have a size and rate budget, and only a school's own
          planner may approve its plan, never one physics hasn't verified. Every AWS call goes
          through an adapter that takes an endpoint, so pointing it at real AWS is a config change.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="run">
      <h2 class="title">Run it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You need <code>uv</code>, <code>just</code> and <code>bun</code>, on Windows, macOS or
          Linux:
        </p>
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/riteshroshann/neem
cd neem
uv sync && just check          # Python: lint, types, tests
cd web && bun install && bun run dev   # the site on :4321
uv run neem-api                # the physics API on :8000</code></pre>
        <p>
          The data pipeline rebuilds everything from public sources: <code>just ingest</code>,
          <code>just scene &lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>just rank</code>, <code>just publish</code>. The
          serverless stack: <code>just localstack</code>, <code>just seed</code>,
          <code>just sam-build</code>, <code>just sam-worker</code>. The planner, with Ollama
          running: <code>just plan osm-w1321574340</code>.
        </p>
        <ul class="tree small">
          <li><code>packages/neem-core</code> physics: scenes, trees and sails, SOLWEIG, the fast preview, the child</li>
          <li><code>packages/neem-data</code> ingest, the scene builder, the city ranking, publishing</li>
          <li><code>packages/neem-agent</code> the Strands planner and its tools</li>
          <li><code>packages/neem-api</code>, <code>neem-worker</code> the service, its queue and its Cedar rules</li>
          <li><code>web/</code> this site: Astro with React for the planner and three.js for 3D</li>
          <li><code>recon/</code> phone video to a 3D model and measured heights</li>
          <li><code>infra/</code> Docker, LocalStack and the SAM template</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="api">
      <h2 class="title">Files and API</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The site needs no server. <code>neem-data publish</code> writes everything it shows as
          plain files, and each school with a twin gets a folder under <code>/twins/&lt;id&gt;/</code>:
        </p>
        <ul class="tree small">
          <li><code>twin.json</code> the school, its grid, and the yard's heat summary for every hour</li>
          <li><code>layers.bin.gz</code> the grids, packed: ground height, roofs, crowns, what counts as yard, and radiant temperature and feels-like for every hour</li>
          <li><code>calibration.json</code> the small per-hour model the instant preview runs on</li>
          <li><code>child.json</code> the nine-year-old's morning, minute by minute</li>
          <li><code>plan.json</code> the agent's plan, its steps and SOLWEIG's verdict</li>
          <li><code>map-1pm*.png</code> the 1 pm heat maps: today, with the plan, and at each modelled age</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The physics API, <code>uv run neem-api</code>, serves the same data and runs the
          verification jobs. Cedar checks every call.
        </p>
        <ul class="tree small">
          <li><code>GET /schools</code>, <code>GET /schools/&lbrace;id&rbrace;</code> the ranking and one school</li>
          <li><code>GET /schools/&lbrace;id&rbrace;/layers.bin</code>, <code>/calibration</code>, <code>/child</code> a twin's files</li>
          <li><code>POST /schools/&lbrace;id&rbrace;/designs</code> trees and sails in, a job id out (202)</li>
          <li><code>GET /jobs/&lbrace;id&rbrace;</code> the job: queued, running, then SOLWEIG's numbers</li>
          <li><code>POST /schools/&lbrace;id&rbrace;/approve</code>, <code>GET /schools/&lbrace;id&rbrace;/approved</code> a school's own planner signs off a verified plan</li>
          <li><code>GET /health</code>, <code>GET /whoami</code> liveness, and who Cedar thinks you are</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="worlds">
      <h2 class="title">Worlds you can walk through</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <figure class="docs-film">
          <video controls muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster="/media/kv-3d.jpg" width="1280" height="800">
            <source src="/media/kv-3d.webm" type="video/webm" />
            <source src="/media/kv-3d.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <figcaption class="xs muted">
            Neem's 3D today: Kendriya Vidyalaya from open data, one column per square metre, the
            ground coloured by SOLWEIG, rendered with three.js.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Neem's 3D today is honest but blocky: one column per square metre, from open data. A real
          yard has a water tank, a stage for assembly, a wall with a crack in it and the exact lean
          of every tree. Two ideas from computer vision close that gap, and they fit together.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>Gaussian splats.</b> A splat scene is a cloud of millions of tiny, soft, coloured blobs.
          Each blob is only a few numbers: where it sits, how it is stretched and turned, its colour,
          and how see-through it is. To draw a frame you sort the blobs by distance and paint them
          back to front. To build a scene you start from a rough point cloud and nudge every number by
          gradient descent until the renders match the photos. No meshes and no textures, just blobs
          that end up looking exactly like the place.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>The plan for <code>recon/</code>.</b> Walk the empty yard with a phone for 60 to 90
          seconds. VGGT, a 3D vision model, turns the frames into camera positions and a dense point
          cloud in one pass. The cloud is levelled, scaled against the mapped buildings and becomes
          measured heights for SOLWEIG. The same footage then trains a splat, and SOLWEIG's map is
          painted onto its ground: you walk through your own school and see where the sun will hurt
          at 1 pm.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>World Labs</b> is taking this furthest. Their world model,
          <a href="https://www.worldlabs.ai/blog/marble-world-model">Marble</a>, generates a
          persistent 3D world from a single image, a text prompt, a video or a rough 3D layout, and
          exports it as Gaussian splats and meshes. Their renderer,
          <a href="https://github.com/sparkjsdev/spark">Spark</a>, is open source under MIT and draws
          splats inside three.js, the engine Neem's 3D already runs on, on WebGL2 phones and headsets;
          Spark 2.0 streams worlds of over 100 million splats into a browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          Neem would use both, kept strictly apart. <b>Measured:</b> a splat of the real yard from a
          phone video, drawn with Spark, every number on it still from physics. <b>Imagined:</b> a
          generated world of the grown grove, seeded from a photo of the yard today, so a principal can
          stand under year thirty before planting year one, labelled as a picture and never as a
          result. Neither is built yet; the phone-to-heights pipeline is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="extend">
      <h2 class="title">Extend it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <b>Another city.</b> Every source is global: Overture, the Meta canopy map, Copernicus,
          OpenStreetMap and ERA5. Change the boundary and the day, and the same commands build its
          schools. <b>Another tree.</b> A species is a height, a crown and how much light the crown
          lets through, set once in Python and once in the browser. <b>Another question.</b> SOLWEIG
          already computes shadow hours and radiant temperature for every cell; a new map is a
          publish step away. <b>Better heights.</b> Open data knows the height of about 0.5% of
          Delhi's buildings; <code>recon/</code> turns a 60 to 90 second phone video into measured
          heights on the same grid.
        </p>
        <p>
          The house rules: every number shown has to come from a run you can repeat, physics code
          keeps its textbook symbols, and a change that weakens a test is a bug.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="further">
      <h2 class="title">How far this can go</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The city ranking ran all {stats.schools} Delhi yards in about ten minutes, and a full 1 m
          twin takes about four seconds a school on one laptop GPU. The Step Functions map in the SAM
          template fans the same job out over a hundred workers, so a state, or a country with about
          15 lakh schools, is a matter of compute, not of new code.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next comes a phone in every yard: a short video becomes a 3D model, measured heights and a
          Gaussian splat you can walk through. Then the same twin every summer, with fresh canopy
          data, to check which saplings made it and what they are doing for the children under them.
          Then the ground beyond schools: bus stops, hospital courtyards, streets and parks. One
          engine, any open ground where people wait in the sun.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule" id="partners">
      <h2 class="title">For NGOs, CSR teams and foundations</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If you plant trees or work with schools, Neem can do the parts that are hard to do by eye.
          <b>Where:</b> rank a district's schools by how hot their yards get for the children in them.
          <b>What:</b> a plan inside your budget, with a verified cooling number, before a single
          sapling goes in. A neem costs about {perTree}; {plan.trees} of them, {rupees(plan.cost_rs)},
          are what the planner chose for Kendriya Vidyalaya. <b>Proof:</b> the same physics after
          planting and year by year as the trees grow, numbers you can put in a donor report and
          defend. <b>Open:</b> the code is GPL and the data is public, so there is nothing to lock
          you in.
        </p>
        <p>
          A simple way to start: pick a district, we run the ranking and the twins, you plant with
          the schools that need it most, and we re-run every summer. Every yard that becomes a grove
          is a classroom for the children who will plant the next one.
        </p>
        <p><a class="button" href={issue}>Start a conversation</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-bottom: var(--s-8);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-6);
  }

  .docs-title {
    max-width: 10ch;
  }

  .toc {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-2) var(--s-5);
  }

  .toc a {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .body {
    padding-bottom: var(--section);
  }

  .block {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 340px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-8);
    padding-block: var(--s-7) var(--s-8);
    scroll-margin-top: 72px;
  }

  .flow {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-5) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }

  .flow li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--s-5);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .flow li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: -5px;
    top: 0.55em;
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .flow b {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 8.5em;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  pre {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0;
    padding: var(--s-4) var(--s-5);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font: 500 var(--t-s) / 1.7 var(--sans);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  code {
    font: 500 0.95em var(--sans);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .tree {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--s-5) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .tree code {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-right: var(--s-2);
  }

  .docs-film {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-6);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
  }

  .docs-film video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    background: #13100d;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--line);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .block {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--s-5);
    }
  }
</style>
